const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Microeconomics studies choices by households and firms. Supply and demand are its core model.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Scarcity means every choice has an opportunity cost. Comparative advantage explains gains from trade."},{"t":"p","text":"Demand slopes down; supply slopes up. A change in price moves along a curve; changes in income, tastes, related prices, expectations or buyers shift demand; input costs, technology and taxes shift supply. Equilibrium sets price and quantity; price ceilings cause shortages and price floors cause surpluses."},{"t":"p","text":"Elasticity: PED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP (use the midpoint formula). Elastic demand (|PED| > 1) means price cuts raise revenue. Tax incidence falls more on the less elastic side."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Price rises from $4 to $6 and quantity falls from 120 to 80. Find PED (midpoint).","steps":["%ΔQ = −40/100 = −40%; %ΔP = 2/5 = 40%"],"answer":"PED = −1 (unit elastic)"}]},{"say":"Firms choose output to maximize profit, and the market structure shapes the outcome.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Costs: fixed, variable, total, average and marginal cost. Diminishing marginal returns make MC rise. Profit is maximized where marginal revenue MR = marginal cost MC. Economic profit subtracts implicit costs; accounting profit doesn't."},{"t":"p","text":"Perfect competition: many firms, identical products, price takers (P = MR); long-run economic profit is zero. Monopoly: one firm, barriers to entry, sets MR = MC then charges the price on demand, causing deadweight loss. Monopolistic competition: differentiated products. Oligopoly: few interdependent firms (game theory, the prisoner's dilemma)."},{"t":"p","text":"Market failures include externalities and public goods; government can use Pigouvian taxes, subsidies and regulation."}]}],[{"say":"Macroeconomics studies the whole economy: total output, jobs and prices.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"GDP = C + I + G + (X − M), the market value of final goods and services produced in a country. Real GDP adjusts for inflation using a deflator; GDP per capita divides by population. GDP misses non-market work, inequality and the environment."},{"t":"p","text":"Unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labor force × 100. Types: frictional, structural and cyclical; the natural rate excludes cyclical."},{"t":"p","text":"Inflation is measured with the CPI: inflation rate = (CPI₂ − CPI₁)/CPI₁ × 100. Unexpected inflation hurts lenders and people on fixed incomes."},{"t":"ex","problem":"The CPI rises from 200 to 210. Find the inflation rate.","steps":["(210 − 200) ÷ 200 × 100"],"answer":"5%"}]},{"say":"Governments and central banks try to keep the economy growing steadily with low inflation.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The AD–AS model: aggregate demand slopes down; short-run aggregate supply slopes up; long-run aggregate supply is vertical at potential output. Recessionary gaps cause unemployment; inflationary gaps cause rising prices."},{"t":"p","text":"Fiscal policy: changes in government spending and taxes. The spending multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC). Deficits add to national debt and may crowd out private investment."},{"t":"p","text":"Monetary policy: the Federal Reserve targets the federal funds rate using interest on reserves and open market operations. Lower rates boost investment and AD; higher rates fight inflation. Banks create money through fractional-reserve lending (money multiplier = 1 ÷ reserve ratio)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"MPC = 0.75. How much can GDP rise from $100 billion of new government spending?","steps":["Multiplier = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4"],"answer":"up to $400 billion"}]}]];
